      "question": {
        ":question/id": "25ddb197-df9c-53bf-b429-458fe757430d",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "$\\int_{5}^{8}\\frac{-4}{x - 4}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x - 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x - 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 5 & 8 \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{5}^{8}\\frac{-4}{x - 4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{-4}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{-4}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =-4\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\ln(u)|_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-4(\\ln(4)-\\ln(1)) \\\\  & =-4\\ln((2)^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 2\\ln(2) \\\\  & =- 8 \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
